-- title:
--   Polynomiality of kernel orders of G(T) on Pic⁰
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a finite field, $K$ an algebraically closed field, and let $F_0$ be a field extension of $k$ and $F$ a field extension of both $K$ and $F_0$, with $F_0$ a curve over $k$ and $F$ a curve over $K$ in the project's sense: every nonzero element has a principal divisor of degree zero whose coefficient at each place is the order of the element there, every place has residue field of finite dimension over the base field, and the module of Kähler differentials is free of rank one over the function field. Assume $F_0$ is generated over $k$ by a finite set, and that $F$ is generated over $K$ by the image of $F_0$. Let $\varphi : F \to F$ be a $K$-algebra endomorphism whose underlying ring homomorphism is integral (so $F$ is integral over its image) and which satisfies $\varphi(x) = x^{\,|k|}$ for $x$ in the image of $F_0$. Let $T$ be an endomorphism of the additive group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of $F/K$ (degree-zero divisors modulo principal ones) which on the class of each degree-zero divisor $D$ is given by the class of the pushforward of $D$ along $\varphi$, that is, each place $w$ of $F$ is sent to its restriction to $F$ along $\varphi$ with multiplicity the inertia degree. Then for every $m \in \mathbb{N}$ there is a polynomial $N \in \mathbb{Z}[x_0,\dots,x_m]$ of total degree at most $2g$, where $g = \mathrm{genusFF}\,K\,F$ is the $K$-dimension of $H^1(0)$, in which the monomial $x_0^{2g}$ has coefficient $1$, such that for every monic $G \in \mathbb{Z}[X]$ of degree $m+1$ whose constant coefficient has nonzero image in $K$, the value of $N$ at the coefficient vector $(G_0,\dots,G_m)$ of $G$ equals the cardinality of the kernel of $G(T)$ acting on $\mathrm{Pic}^0$, as an integer (and hence $0$ when that kernel is infinite).
--
--   This is the polynomiality of the degree on the commutative subring $\mathbb{Z}[T]$ of $\mathrm{End}\,\mathrm{Pic}^0$ generated by the relative Frobenius, read on those elements $G(T)$ whose constant coefficient is invertible in $K$: classically the order of the kernel is $\prod_i G(\omega_i)$ over the roots $\omega_i$ of the characteristic polynomial of Frobenius, which accounts for the degree bound $2g$ and the normalisation of the leading coefficient in $x_0$. It is used by the companion result expressing this kernel order as the absolute value of a resultant, on the way to the Riemann hypothesis for curves over finite fields.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Pic0_exists_mvPolynomial_eval_eq_natCard_ker_aeval_of_pushforwardAlong_frobenius.lean

